<title-group>
<article-title>Deficit irrigation of field tomatoes: yield and water use over two seasons</article-title>
</title-group>
<contrib-group>
<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Halvorsen</surname><given-names>Ingrid</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib>
<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Okafor</surname><given-names>Chidi</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib>
</contrib-group>
<aff id="aff1"><label>1</label><institution>Northfield Agricultural Institute</institution><country>Norway</country></aff>
<aff id="aff2"><label>2</label><institution>Coastal Plains Research Station</institution><country>Nigeria</country></aff>
<abstract>
<p>Deficit irrigation supplied 70% of crop evapotranspiration to field tomatoes over the 2024 and 2025 seasons. Marketable yield fell by 6% against full irrigation, while water use fell by 28%, so that water productivity rose from 18.4 to 23.9 kg per cubic metre.</p>
</abstract>
<kwd-group><kwd>deficit irrigation</kwd><kwd>tomato</kwd><kwd>water productivity</kwd></kwd-group>
</article-meta>
</front>
<body>
<sec id="s1">
<title>Introduction</title>
<p>Irrigation accounts for most of the fresh water withdrawn in the study region, and the allocations to farms were cut in 2023. Deficit irrigation, which supplies less water than the crop uses, has kept yields of other crops close to those of full irrigation <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib1">[1]</xref>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="s2">
<title>Materials and methods</title>
<sec id="s2-1">
<title>Study site</title>
<p>The trial was run at the Northfield station on a sandy loam soil, in a randomized complete block design with four blocks.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="s2-2">
<title>Treatments</title>
<p>Three treatments were compared:</p>
<list list-type="bullet">
<list-item><p>full irrigation, at 100% of crop evapotranspiration;</p></list-item>
<list-item><p>deficit irrigation, at 70% from flowering to harvest;</p></list-item>
<list-item><p>rain-fed plots, without irrigation.</p></list-item>
</list>
</sec>
</sec>
<sec id="s3">
<title>Results</title>
<p>The rain-fed plots yielded least in both seasons (Table 1).</p>
<table-wrap id="tbl1">
<label>Table 1.</label>
<caption><title>Marketable yield and water use by treatment, mean of the two seasons.</title></caption>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Yield (t/ha)</th><th>Water use (mm)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Full irrigation</td><td>84.2</td><td>457</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deficit irrigation</td><td>79.1</td><td>331</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain-fed</td><td>41.6</td><td>212</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1"><p>Water use includes the rainfall of the season.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot>
</table-wrap>
<fig id="fig1">
<label>Figure 1.</label>
<caption><title>Water productivity of the three treatments in each season.</title></caption>
</fig>
</sec>
<sec id="s4">
<title>Discussion</title>
<p>The loss of yield under deficit irrigation was small against the water saved, and the fruit of the deficit plots had a higher sugar content.</p>
</sec>
</body>
